import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var i={prism:{theory:{concept:"[[Dispersion of light is the dependence of a medium's refractive index on the colour (frequency) of light; because of it, white light splits into a spectrum.]] A prism refracts the light twice, at two tilted faces, so the colours spread further apart and white light fans out into a spectrum from red to violet. The colours of a rainbow appear in the same way, through dispersion in water droplets."},section:{dispersion:{title:"White light is a mixture",text:"Sunlight or the light of a white lamp contains all colours. In the simulation the white beam is represented by seven colours from red (656 nm) to violet (405 nm). [[Inside the prism they separate: red is turned least, violet most.]] On the screen they land side by side as a spectrum: red, orange, yellow, green, cyan, blue, violet."},why:{title:"Why colours separate",text:"[[The refractive index n tells how many times slower light is in a material: v = c / n.]] In crown glass n is 1.514 for red light and 1.530 for violet, so violet travels a little slower and bends a little more at each face. Watch the coloured pulses: inside the prism the violet pulse falls a little behind the red one (only about 1 %, so look closely). In a vacuum all colours travel at the same speed c, so there is no dispersion."},deviation:{title:"The angle of deviation",text:"The dashed line shows where the light would go without the prism. The angle between it and the leaving ray is the deviation \u03B4. Light is always turned towards the thicker part of the prism (its base). A larger apex angle or a material with a larger refractive index turns the light more. If i is the angle of incidence and e the angle at which light leaves, \u03B4 = i + e \u2212 A."},minimum:{title:"Minimum deviation",text:"Change the angle of incidence slowly and watch \u03B4: it first falls, then rises again. It is smallest when the light crosses the prism symmetrically, parallel to the base, with e = i. From this minimum deviation the refractive index can be measured very accurately: n = sin((\u03B4_min + A)/2) / sin(A/2). This is how optical glass is tested."},inside:{title:"When light cannot get out",text:"At the second face light goes from glass into air. If it meets the face too steeply (more than the critical angle \u03B8_c = asin(1/n) from the normal), it is totally reflected back into the glass. Because violet has the larger n, it is trapped first: there is an angle where red still escapes but violet does not. In diamond (n \u2248 2.42) the critical angle is only 24\xB0, so with a 60\xB0 prism no light gets through the second face at any angle; jewellers cut diamonds so that light bounces inside and leaves as coloured sparkles."},rainbow:{title:"Rainbows and everyday dispersion",text:"A rainbow is dispersion in water drops: sunlight is refracted on entering a drop, reflected at its back and refracted again on leaving, so each colour leaves at a slightly different angle (about 42\xB0 for red, 40\xB0 for violet). Dispersion is also why cheap lenses show coloured fringes; camera lenses combine crown and flint glass to cancel it. Flint glass disperses about twice as strongly as crown glass, which shows in its smaller Abbe number V."},newton:{title:"Newton's experiment",text:"In 1666 Isaac Newton let a narrow beam of sunlight through a prism and saw a long coloured band. Then he let only one colour pass into a second prism: it was bent again but did not split any more. So the prism does not create the colours \u2014 they are already in white light, and the prism only separates them. Newton's colour names (with 'indigo' between blue and violet) are still used today."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"Which colour is turned most by a glass prism? (Violet.) Why? (Its refractive index is larger, because it travels more slowly in glass.) Does a prism make colours? (No, it separates the colours already in white light.) What happens to \u03B4 when you turn the prism towards minimum deviation? (It becomes smallest when the path is symmetric.) Why does no light leave a 60\xB0 diamond prism through its second face? (The angle there is always above diamond's small critical angle.) Try each one in the simulation."}},formula:{snell:"Snell's law at a face (light in air, angles from the normal)",speed:"Speed of light in the material",deviation:"Deviation: angle of incidence + angle of exit \u2212 apex angle",angles:"Inside the prism the two refraction angles add up to the apex angle",minimum:"Refractive index from the minimum deviation",cauchy:"Cauchy's formula: n is larger for shorter wavelengths",abbe:"Abbe number: the smaller V, the stronger the dispersion"},variable:{deviation:"Angle of deviation",index:"Refractive index"},assumption:{rays:"Geometrical optics: thin rays, no diffraction. White light is represented by seven wavelengths, not by its full continuous spectrum.",main:"Only the main ray is followed. The weak partial reflection at each face and absorption in the material are not shown.",cauchy:"The refractive index follows Cauchy's formula fitted to catalogue values of each material (N-BK7, F2, water at 20 \xB0C, diamond).",slow:"The light pulses move enormously slowed down, but with the true ratio of speeds: n times slower inside the material than in air.",scale:"The prism, the beam and the distance to the screen are not to scale."},param:{incidence:"Angle of incidence",material:"Material",apex:"Apex angle of the prism",light:"Light",wavelength:"Wavelength",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{incidence:"The angle between the beam and the normal (the dashed line at right angles to the face).",material:"Different materials slow light down by different amounts \u2014 and disperse colours differently.",apex:"The angle at the top of the prism, between the two faces the light passes through.",light:"White light contains all colours; single-colour light (like a laser) contains just one.",wavelength:"The colour of the light: about 700 nm is red, 400 nm violet."},preset:{apex30:"30\xB0",apex45:"45\xB0",apex60:"60\xB0",red:"Red 656 nm",sodium:"Yellow 589 nm",green:"Green laser 532 nm",violet:"Violet 405 nm"},material:{crown:"Crown glass (N-BK7)",flint:"Flint glass (F2)",water:"Water",diamond:"Diamond"},light:{white:"White light",single:"One colour"},measure:{deviation:"Deviation of yellow (or the chosen) light",spread:"Width of the spectrum",index:"Refractive index (yellow or chosen light)",indexRed:"Refractive index for red",indexViolet:"Refractive index for violet",exitAngle:"Angle of exit",minDeviation:"Minimum deviation",minIncidence:"Angle of incidence for minimum deviation",criticalAngle:"Critical angle inside",speed:"Speed of light in the material",abbe:"Abbe number"},graph:{deviation:"Deviation vs angle of incidence",red:"Red",violet:"Violet"},view:{white:"white light",single:"light {wavelength}",trappedAll:"total internal reflection",trappedSome:"{count} colour(s) totally reflected",deviation:"deviation \u03B4 = {value}",noDeviation:"no light leaves through the second face",spread:"spectrum {value} wide",colour:{red:"red",orange:"orange",yellow:"yellow",green:"green",cyan:"cyan",blue:"blue",violet:"violet"},pulsesOption:"Light pulses",screenOption:"Screen",normalsOption:"Normals",straightOption:"Path without the prism"},challenge:{wide:{title:"A wide rainbow",description:"Spread white light into a spectrum at least 3\xB0 wide.",hint:"Try another material, or turn the prism so that the light leaves the second face at a steep angle."},bend:{title:"Bend it a lot",description:"Turn the light by at least 50\xB0.",hint:"A material with a larger refractive index bends light more."},least:{title:"The least bending",description:"Find the angle of incidence at which the light is turned least (within 1\xB0).",hint:"Change the angle in small steps and watch \u03B4: it falls, then rises again."},trap:{title:"Trap the light",description:"Make at least one colour totally reflected at the second face.",hint:"Make the angle of incidence small, or the apex angle large."},redOnly:{title:"Red gets out, violet does not",description:"With white light, let red leave through the second face while violet is totally reflected.",hint:"Violet has the larger n, so its critical angle is smaller. Lower the angle of incidence very slowly."},thin:{title:"Measure like an optician",description:"Set the apex angle to 45\xB0 and turn the prism to minimum deviation (within 0.5\xB0).",hint:"At minimum deviation the path inside is parallel to the base and e = i."}}}},a={sim:i};export{a as default,i as sim};
